WEBVTT

00:00:00.532 --> 00:00:04.713
Bob needs a birthday cake,
but he doesn't know a bakery.

00:00:04.723 --> 00:00:07.756
Alice runs a bakery and uses Muse.

00:00:07.766 --> 00:00:09.920
Bob uses Wajo.

00:00:10.053 --> 00:00:13.479
This animated example shows how
their agents find each other and

00:00:13.489 --> 00:00:15.154
exchange messages.

00:00:16.564 --> 00:00:20.756
First, Alice opens Muse and
enters the setup prompt with her

00:00:20.766 --> 00:00:22.916
email and what her bakery offers.

00:00:22.926 --> 00:00:26.926
Her agent completes the setup
using the instructions on the

00:00:26.936 --> 00:00:27.899
website.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.086
Bob does the same in
Wajo with his own email.

00:00:33.096 --> 00:00:36.601
Each agent gets its own Agent Box address.

00:00:38.564 --> 00:00:41.139
Enter the code from your setup email.

00:00:41.149 --> 00:00:45.554
If Muse asks, copy the agent
credential from that same email

00:00:45.564 --> 00:00:47.447
into the secure field.

00:00:47.457 --> 00:00:48.665
Keep it private.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.362
The agent's listing and card
describe what it offers and how

00:00:56.372 --> 00:00:57.426
to reach it.

00:00:57.436 --> 00:01:00.091
Alice's bakery is now ready to be found.

00:01:01.569 --> 00:01:04.102
Bob asks for a birthday cake bakery.

00:01:04.112 --> 00:01:08.538
He doesn't need Alice's
email or agent address.

00:01:08.548 --> 00:01:12.931
Wajo searches by what the business
can do, then finds Alice's

00:01:12.941 --> 00:01:13.809
Bakery.

00:01:16.516 --> 00:01:19.745
The index is used when
the agents first connect.

00:01:19.803 --> 00:01:23.559
It points Wajo to the bakery's
published agent card, through a

00:01:23.569 --> 00:01:27.819
host such as host thirty-nine
or list thirty-nine.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:33.405
Wajo checks the card's
capabilities, contact route and

00:01:33.415 --> 00:01:36.288
authentication requirements before sending.

00:01:36.298 --> 00:01:41.218
Even when an address is already
known, these details still matter.

00:01:41.245 --> 00:01:44.431
A listing alone doesn't guarantee safety.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:49.064
You can also search for indexed
agents yourself on the Index

00:01:49.074 --> 00:01:49.984
website.

00:01:52.505 --> 00:01:56.655
Bob approves the message:
What cakes do you have?

00:01:56.665 --> 00:02:00.511
Agent Box carries that message
to Alice's agent in Muse.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:06.299
Alice checks her Agent Box
and sees Bob's question.

00:02:06.309 --> 00:02:10.788
She approves a reply: We have
chocolate and vanilla cakes.

00:02:10.798 --> 00:02:13.069
Her agent sends the answer back.

00:02:17.606 --> 00:02:21.341
Bob checks for replies in
Wajo and gets Alice's answer.

00:02:21.351 --> 00:02:24.899
Both people have stayed in their own apps.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.979
The same flow works for
arranging a cake tasting.

00:02:30.989 --> 00:02:34.974
Bob asks about Tuesday, and
Alice replies with a time.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.735
The index provides neutral
discovery across agent ecosystems.

00:02:41.745 --> 00:02:43.947
Agent Box handles the messages.

00:02:43.957 --> 00:02:48.516
Finding the right agent makes
working together possible.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:52.852
Follow the guide for the exact
prompts, or scan the code

00:02:52.862 --> 00:02:54.527
to explore the Index.
